\n## Motivation\n\nThe determinant of a nonsingular integer matrix has absolute value at least one. The source examines the proposed counterpart for integer matrices over group rings, where the determinant is defined by an operator trace. The source is OpenAI's September 2026 manuscript.\n\n## Setting\n\nThe left regular operator associated to a group-ring matrix acts on $\\ell^2(G)^n$ℓ 2(G)n by finite convolution. Its Fuglede–Kadison determinant in the invertible case is the exponential of half the real trace of the logarithm of its positive square.\n\n## Formalization targets\n\n$$\\exists A\\in M_n(\\mathbb Z[G]),\\quad A\\text{ invertible over }\\mathbb Q[G],\\quad 0<\\det_{\\mathcal N(G)}(T_A)<1.$$∃A∈M n​(Z[G]),A invertible over Q[G],0<N(G)det​(T A​)<1.\n\nThe defined proposition MainStatement holds, i.e. there exist a group G that is finitely generated, an integer n ≥ 1, an n×n matrix A with entries in the integral group ring ℤ[G], and a bounded ℂ-linear operator T on the Hilbert space l²(G)^n (square-summable complex functions on Fin n × G) such that four conditions hold. First, the image of A in the rational group ring ℚ[G] (obtained by applying the integer-to-rational map to every coefficient) is an invertible matrix over ℚ[G]. Second, T is exactly the left-regular operator of A, meaning (Tξ)(i,h) = Σ_j Σ_g a_{ij}(g) ξ(j, g⁻¹h), where a_{ij}(g) ∈ ℤ is the coefficient of g in the entry A_{ij}. Third, T is invertible as a bounded operator on l²(G)^n. Fourth, its Fuglede–Kadison determinant fkDet(T) = exp(Re tr(log(T*T))/2) satisfies 0 < fkDet(T) < 1, where tr(S) = Σ_i ⟨e_i, S e_i⟩ is the unnormalized trace summing over the basis vectors e_i supported at the identity element of G in component i, and log is the continuous functional calculus logarithm.\n\nThe goal is `OAI.GroupRingDeterminant.main`.\n\n## Significance\n\nThe goal gives a square-matrix obstruction with both a rational group-ring inverse and a bounded operator inverse. This keeps the logarithm in the invertible setting. The manuscript presents a mathematical argument; the attached goal currently has status Open, so the requested contribution is a Lean proof of the displayed formal target.\n\n## Difficulty\n\nThe trace logarithm is not an ordinary finite-dimensional determinant polynomial. The algebraic matrix and bounded regular operator must be shown to match exactly before the determinant estimate is meaningful.\n\n## Formalization scope\n\nThe group is finitely generated and $n\\ge1$n≥1. Lean uses complex square-summable functions on `Fin n × G`, the unnormalized matrix trace, and continuous functional calculus for the logarithm. Invertibility is explicit on both the rational matrix and the operator.\n\nThe shared definitions are supplied by `GroupRingDeterminant`. The target uses Lean 4.33.1 with the pinned Mathlib environment. Contributions should preserve the existing statement and develop reusable supporting lemmas in its definitions.\n\n## Selected references\n\n  * OpenAI, A Counterexample to the Group-Ring Determinant Conjecture, preprint, September 2026. Manuscript.\n  * OpenAI, accompanying Lean statement. Pinned source.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nResearch (Igor Pak Home Page) (https://www.math.ucla.edu/~pak/papers/research.htm)\nciteturn15search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago;   * (with Nikita Gladkov and Aleksandr Zimin) The bunkbed conjecture is false, Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences USA (PNAS), vol. 122 (2025), no. 24, paper e2420725122, 11 pp.\n\nWe give an explicit counterexample to the bunkbed conjecture introduced by Kasteleyn in 1985.\nThe proof uses a connection with continued fractions, \"thin orbits\", and Zaremba's conjecture.\n\nDownload the .pdf file of the paper.\nHere is Alex's tweet on the paper.\n\nSee my talk on the paper, video and slides.\nSee also Alex's talk on the paper (video and slides).\nAdditionally, see SH's talk on the paper (video and slides).\n\nNote: Our estimates on the Hausdorff dimension of the Hančl�Turek fractal was improved to 20 significant digits by Mark Pollicott in this paper.\n\n  * (with Nikita Gladkov and Aleksandr Zimin) The bunkbed conjecture is false, Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences USA (PNAS), vol. 122 (2025), no. 24, paper e2420725122, 11 pp.\n\nWe give an explicit counterexample to the bunkbed conjecture introduced by Kasteleyn in 1985. The counterexample is given by a planar graph on 7222 vertices, and is built on the recent work Hollom (2024).\n\nDownload .pdf file of the original version, or the published version (with a much cleaned up proof).\nSupplementary materials and computer calculations are available on GitHub.\nSee also `Lean` verification here.\n\nSee my blog post on the paper and some backstory in this blog post.\nSee Nikita's talk on the paper in the context of his thesis.\nSee also slides of my talk on the story of the BBC (both before and after our paper).\n\nSee also this series of tweets by Tim Gowers, UCLA press release, this YouTube video by Trefor Bazett or that YouTube video (in German) by Johann Beurich.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nwhataifound.org: What AI Has Actually Contributed | Registry of AI Scientific & Math Results (https://whataifound.org/?tag=regularity)\nciteturn15search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; ## Counterexample to the Jacobian conjecture in dimension three#\n\nAn explicit polynomial map in three variables with constant Jacobian determinant $-2$ that is nevertheless not invertible, disproving a conjecture open since 1939. ... A separate question was opened in September 2026, about provenance rather than priority of the counterexample: the supplemental statement on page 160 of arXiv:2609.05746 asserts prior systematic use of the $xy$, $1+xy$ and $\\delta+xy$ constructions, including the map to the projective line given by $(x : 1+xy)$, in an in-progress monograph circulated publicly in January 2025.\n\nImageAnthropic\n\n2026-07-19\n\nFormally verified Collaborative\n\nModel\n    Claude Fable 5\n\nField\n    mathematics\n\nPosed\n    1939 · open 87 yrs\n\n## Counterexample to the Jacobian conjecture in dimension three#\n\nAn explicit polynomial map in three variables with constant Jacobian determinant $-2$ that is nevertheless not invertible, disproving a conjecture open since 1939.\n\nThe map $$F(x,y,z) = \\bigl(u^3z + y^2u(4+3xy),\\ y + 3xu^2z + 3xy^2(4+3xy),\\ 2x - 3x^2y - x^3z\\bigr)$$ with $u = 1+xy$ has Jacobian determinant identically $-2$, yet sends the three distinct points $(0, 0, -1/4)$, $(1, -3/2, 13/2)$ and $(-1, 3/2, 13/2)$ all to $(-1/4, 0, 0)$. A map with a global inverse cannot be three-to-one. Alpöge, a number theorist at Anthropic, announced it on X the day it was found.\n\nWith Levent Alpöge\n\nalgebraic-geometrycounterexamplepolynomial-maps\n\nOriginal work\n    jacobianfun.org↗ulam.ai↗\n\nCommentary\n    Alexis Gallagher↗Hacker News↗arXiv↗\n\nChallenge\n    arXiv↗\n\nNovelty check, caveats & sources\n\nNovelty check\n\nThe Jacobian conjecture (Keller, 1939) has been a celebrated open problem for 87 years, with many published false proofs in both directions. No prior counterexample in any dimension over characteristic 0 exists in the literature. Ott-Heinrich Keller's original formulation is the one addressed. A separate question was opened in September 2026, about provenance rather than priority of the counterexample: the supplemental statement on page 160 of arXiv:2609.05746 asserts prior systematic use of the $xy$, $1+xy$ and $\\delta+xy$ constructions, including the map to the projective line given by $(x : 1+xy)$, in an in-progress monograph circulated publicly in January 2025. That bears on where the construction came from, not on whether a counterexample already existed, and the caveats record what is and is not claimed.\nWhat he does not claim is that the counterexample itself appears in the earlier work, and he notes that one of the overlapping constructions plays no role in the counterexamples. So this is a question about where the construction came from and what was disclosed, not a challenge to whether the map is a counterexample, and the formal grade is unaffected. It is not a generic complaint either: $(x : 1+xy)$ is the device the seven-page paper's own abstract is built on. In September 2026 Arno van den Essen, a specialist in the conjecture, posted an elementary derivation of a counterexample (arXiv:2609.17795, 15 September 2026) whose abstract states that it is, apart from linear coordinate changes, the example found by Alpoge. That corroborates the counterexample independently of this lab, and it also bears on the priority question above in both directions: it shows the example is reachable by elementary mathematics, which cuts against the need for any particular source, while saying nothing about what the model was given. It does not mention Claude or Anthropic.\n\nIndependent checks\n\nwhataifound.org (symbolic recomputation, SymPy): confirmed, $\\det J = -2$ identically; all three points map to $(-1/4, 0, 0)$\n\nMultiple mathematicians via public computer-algebra checks: confirmed · link ↗\n\nArno van den Essen: Derives a counterexample to the Jacobian conjecture by elementary means and states that it is, apart from linear coordinate changes, the example found by Alpoge. Independent corroboration of the counterexample by a specialist in the conjecture; it does not audit the model's role. · link ↗\n\nIN Independent\n\n2026-07-14\n\nFormally verified AI-led\n\nModel\n    GPT-5.6 Sol Pro\n\nField\n    mathematics\n\nPosed\n    1996 · open 30 yrs\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n4  P. Beelen and D. Ruano (https://sites.cs.ucsb.edu/~rich/publications/sullivan.pdf)\nciteturn15search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 17.4 years ago; Crawled: 17.4 years ago; Beelen and D. ... To bound the minimum d